<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The FIR dated to 2013 — allegations of cruelty and criminal breach of trust against the husband and his family. Over the following decade the matrimonial relationship ran its full legal course: the wife pursued a case under the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act; the marriage was dissolved by an ex parte decree in September 2025; and in the DV proceedings, mediation produced a settlement of Rs 10 lakh, upon which the wife withdrew the DV case in August 2026. With the financial and matrimonial accounts closed, the mother-in-law and brothers-in-law sought quashing of the criminal case.</p>

<h2>Genuine, voluntary — and comprehensive</h2>
<p>The court satisfied itself on the two questions that decide settlement-based quashing. First, the quality of the settlement: reached in court-annexed mediation within the DV case, acted upon by payment, and followed by withdrawal of the DV proceedings.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;The settlement is genuine and voluntary.&rdquo; And on continuation: &ldquo;No useful purpose would be served by continuing the proceedings against them and their continuance would amount to an abuse of the process of the Court.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>Second, the nature of the offence. Following <em>Gian Singh</em>, <em>K. Srinivas Rao</em> and <em>Laxmi Narayan</em>, matrimonial offences arising out of a private relationship are the paradigm case for settlement-based quashing: once the parties have genuinely closed their dispute, the criminal process serves no public interest and burdens both sides. A 2013 FIR prosecuted into 2026, after divorce and a settled DV case, fit that description precisely — for those who had faced the process.</p>

<h2>The absconder exception</h2>
<p>The husband got nothing. He was absconding, and the court confined the relief to the family members who had submitted to the law. The exclusion is worth noting as a rule of practice: quashing under the inherent jurisdiction is equitable and discretionary, and a petitioner — or co-accused — who evades process cannot claim its benefit on the strength of a settlement others honoured. If he surfaces, he must face the proceedings and make his own case.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For settling families.</strong> A mediation settlement inside DV or maintenance proceedings can be drafted to resolve everything — the DV case, the FIR, stridhan and alimony. Make the criminal-case closure an express term, and route the quashing petition promptly after payment.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For complainant wives.</strong> The settlement&rsquo;s terms are the protection. Payment before or at quashing, recorded in the order, is the standard Delhi practice; this case followed the familiar sequence of payment, withdrawal, then quashing.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For absconding accused.</strong> Evasion forfeits the shortcut. The route back runs through appearance — anticipatory bail or surrender — and only then a quashing plea on the settlement.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s dockets carry hundreds of aging 498A FIRs attached to marriages that have long since ended, and the High Court&rsquo;s settlement-quashing jurisprudence is the mechanism that retires them. This order is a conventional application with one instructive refinement: relief follows participation. The in-laws who faced the process for thirteen years leave it; the husband who fled remains in it. For practitioners structuring matrimonial settlements, the case is a current reminder that mediation in the DV case can be the engine for closing the criminal file — and that every intended beneficiary of that closure must be before the court.</p>
